The KN5000 panel occasionally responded to a press with the wrong function and
put a phantom `<Db>` transpose on the home screen that nobody asked for. The bug
is in the SHARED TMP94C241 synchronous-serial model, not any button table (the
SEG.bit map, layout inputtags and cpanel dispatch are byte-identical to upstream).

tmp94c241_serial_device::sioclk() latched an RX byte and fired INTRX on EVERY 8
rising edges, regardless of which side drove the clock. While the main CPU is the
clock master transmitting a command byte (internal baud generator, IOC=0), those
same 8 edges also completed a bogus full-duplex "RX during TX" byte from the idle
RXD line — one phantom RX byte + spurious INTRX for every command byte the CPU
sent. A phantom 0x00 decodes as right-panel segment 0 = the TRANSPOSE row (the
`<Db>`); an odd phantom count inside a framed burst swaps the (header,state)
pairing, so PIANO (seg2,0x01) reads as (seg1,0x02) = ORCHESTRAL PAD. Timing-
dependent, hence intermittent.

Behavioural oracle diff (instrument both ends): the cpanel send_byte() count
exactly equals the externally clocked (IOC=1) RX bytes — 190==190 — while every
phantom is internally clocked with IOC=0 (521 of them): a perfect partition with
zero cross terms. IOC is the firmware's own receive-enable (it sets IOC=1 in the
INTRX1 ISR, the same condition that already gates the internal baud generator off
in timer_callback); the receiver simply wasn't honouring that gate.

Fix: in sioclk()'s rising-edge RX block, count/latch bits only when the external
clock is actually selected: (SCxMOD transfer==0) && IOC. After the fix, sent ==
received (96==96, all IOC=1), phantoms 521->0, no phantom transpose, and every
silk-labelled press opens its promised screen (PIANO->SOUND PIANO, DISK->DISK
MENU, MENU:SOUND->SOUND MENU, MENU:MIDI->MIDI, POP&BALLAD->RHYTHM POP, PART
SELECT) from a clean home savestate at three different emulated phases each.

UART transfer modes drive their RX from timer_callback, so this synchronous-mode
gate never touches the MIDI channel. KN5000/KN1500 device only (KN7000/KN6000 are
MN10300 and don't use this device). -validate clean for kn5000/kn1500/kn7000/
kn6000/kn6500/kn2400; sound names (kn5000-29) and tempo wheel (kn5000-25) intact.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/devices/cpu/tlcs900/tmp94c241_serial.cpp | 21 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/devices/cpu/tlcs900/tmp94c241_serial.cpp b/src/devices/cpu/tlcs900/tmp94c241_serial.cpp
index db123ef..fb38f97 100644
--- a/src/devices/cpu/tlcs900/tmp94c241_serial.cpp
+++ b/src/devices/cpu/tlcs900/tmp94c241_serial.cpp
@@ -168,7 +168,26 @@ void tmp94c241_serial_device::sioclk(int state)
 			}
 		}
 
-		if (m_rx_clock_count)
+		// Only latch RX bits that are clocked by an EXTERNAL master.  In
+		// I/O-interface mode (SCxMOD transfer=0) the control panel drives SCLK
+		// for its INTA responses, and the firmware selects the external clock
+		// by setting IOC (SCxCR bit0) in its receive ISR — the SAME condition
+		// that gates the internal baud generator off (see timer_callback).
+		//
+		// While the CPU is the clock master (IOC=0, internal baud generator
+		// running) it is TRANSMITTING commands; the RXD line then carries no
+		// framed slave byte.  Latching those full-duplex "RX during TX" edges
+		// manufactured a phantom RX byte + a spurious INTRX for EVERY command
+		// byte the CPU sent — the root cause of the control-panel "scrambled
+		// buttons" and the unrequested phantom transpose (a phantom 0x00 byte
+		// decodes as right-panel segment 0 = the TRANSPOSE row; an odd count of
+		// phantom bytes inside a framed burst swaps the header/state pairing).
+		// The sender (cpanel) is correct; the receiver was over-counting edges.
+		// Mirror the sender's start-gate: count RX bits only when the external
+		// clock is actually selected.  (UART transfer modes drive their RX from
+		// timer_callback, not here, so this synchronous gate never affects them.)
+		const bool ext_clock = ((m_serial_mode & 0x03) == 0) && BIT(m_serial_control, 0);
+		if (m_rx_clock_count && ext_clock)
 		{
 			m_rx_clock_count--;
 
